To create a link for every post, you'll need to modify your configuration to generate a ` Perkins-Link` page, also known as a "PermaLink" or "Canonical Link".

The `try_files` directive in your `location /` block is already directing requests to `index.html`, which can include the links to individual posts. However, you'll need to add a new location block to serve the links to each post.

Assuming you have a structure like this:

* `index.html` contains a list of post links (e.g., `<a href="/post-1">Post 1</a>`, `<a href="/post-2">Post 2</a>`, etc.)
* Each post is stored in a separate file in the `/usr/share/nginx/html/posts` directory (e.g., `post-1.html`, `post-2.html`, etc.)

You can add a new location block to serve the links to each post:
```nginx
location ~* "^/post-\d+\.html$" {
    root /usr/share/nginx/html/posts;
    try_files $uri =404;
}
```
This location block will match any URL that starts with `/post-` followed by one or more digits (`\d+`) and ends with `.html`. It will serve the corresponding file from the `/usr/share/nginx/html/posts` directory.

To create the link for each post, you can add a `Link` header to the `index.html` file. For example, you can add the following HTML snippet to create a link for each post:
```html
<a href="/post-{{post.id}}">Post {{post.title}}</a>
```
Replace `post.id` and `post.title` with the actual values from your data.

In your `index.html` file, you'll also need to include the `Link` header to tell search engines and other crawlers about the association between the post links and the corresponding HTML files. You can add the following meta tag:
```html
<link rel="canonical" href="/post-{{post.id}}.html">
```
This will tell search engines and crawlers that the `index.html` page is a hub for links to individual posts, and that the canonical version of each post is the corresponding HTML file.

By following these steps, you should be able to create links for every post and make them accessible through URLs like `/post-1`, `/post-2`, etc.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

